README.style
------------

Hackedbox Styles
================

A Hackedbox style is an X resource file containing the visual configuration
used by the window manager. Styles control the appearance of menus, window
decorations, fonts, colors, textures, borders, and the root background.

Styles are plain text files and can be created or modified with any text
editor.


Style Locations
===============

System-installed styles are located in:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/styles/

User styles may be placed in:

    ~/.hackedbox/styles/

A style may also be stored elsewhere on the filesystem. Hackedbox only
requires read access to the file.


Loading a Style
===============

The current style is selected through the Hackedbox menu or configuration.

When a style is loaded, Hackedbox reads the X resources contained in the
style file and applies the settings to the window manager.

Styles can be changed without rebuilding Hackedbox.


Style File Structure
====================

A style consists of resource keys and values:

    resource.key: value

Resource names are separated by periods. X resource wildcards may also be
used where supported.

Comments begin with:

    !

For example:

    ! Menu title
    menu.title.font: Hack-14
    menu.title.textColor: white


Fonts
=====

Fonts are assigned to individual style components.

Examples:

    menu.title.font: Hack-14
    menu.frame.font: Hack-10

Use a font name or alias supported by the X11/Xft font system used by the
current Hackedbox build.

Fonts do not have to be identical throughout the style. Different
components may use different families and sizes.


Menus
=====

The menu system is divided into the title, frame, and highlighted item.

Typical resources include:

    menu.title.font
    menu.title
    menu.title.color
    menu.title.colorTo
    menu.title.textColor
    menu.title.justify

    menu.frame.font
    menu.frame
    menu.frame.color
    menu.frame.colorTo
    menu.frame.textColor
    menu.frame.justify

    menu.hilite
    menu.hilite.color
    menu.hilite.colorTo
    menu.hilite.textColor

Menu bullets can be configured with:

    menu.bullet
    menu.bullet.position


Textures
========

Textures determine how a component is drawn.

The texture description can specify:

    raised
    sunken
    flat

along with:

    solid
    gradient

and gradient direction where supported.

Bevel settings determine how the edge shading is rendered.

Bevel1 places the shading at the edge of the component.

Bevel2 places the shading one pixel inward from the edge.

Texture keywords are not required to use uppercase characters.


Colors
======

Colors may be specified using standard X color names or X color
specifications.

Examples:

    white
    black
    green
    rgb:90/94/98
    #08FF52

Gradient textures use both:

    color
    colorTo

Solid textures only require the primary color.


Window Decorations
==================

Window decorations have separate focused and unfocused states.

The title, label, buttons, frame, handle, and grip can each have their own
appearance and colors.

The relevant resources are grouped under the window namespace, for example:

    window.title.focus
    window.title.focus.color
    window.title.focus.colorTo

    window.title.unfocus
    window.title.unfocus.color
    window.title.unfocus.colorTo

    window.label.focus
    window.label.unfocus

    window.button.focus
    window.button.unfocus
    window.button.pressed

    window.frame.focusColor
    window.frame.unfocusColor

    window.handle.focus
    window.handle.unfocus

    window.grip.focus
    window.grip.unfocus

Text and button colors are configured through the corresponding resources.


Borders and Geometry
====================

The following resources control the physical dimensions of decorations:

    borderWidth
    bevelWidth
    handleWidth

The border color is controlled by:

    borderColor

For example:

    borderColor: green
    borderWidth: 1
    bevelWidth: 2
    handleWidth: 5


Window Layout and Justification
===============================

Text justification can be configured with:

    window.justify

Menu and title components may also provide their own justification
resources.

Supported values depend on the component and current style parser.


Backgrounds
==========

Hackedbox uses hbsetbg for root background handling.

New styles should use:

    root.background: /path/to/image.jpg
    root.background.mode: center

For example:

    root.background: /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/default.jpg
    root.background.mode: center

User backgrounds may also be stored under:

    ~/.hackedbox/backgrounds/

The optional background rotation resources are:

    root.background.folder
    root.background.timer

A timer value of 0 disables rotation.

The older:

    rootCommand:

resource is retained for compatibility with older styles but is not used by
the current root background system. New styles should use the
root.background resources instead.


Pointers and Cursors
====================

Mouse pointer handling is provided by X11 rather than by the Hackedbox
style texture system.

Cursor themes and sizes can therefore be configured through the X cursor
resources, for example:

    Xcursor.theme: breeze_cursors
    Xcursor.size: 26

These settings normally belong in the user's X resource configuration
rather than in the Hackedbox style.


Creating a Style From Scratch
=============================

The simplest way to create a new style is to start with an empty file and
add only the resources that need to be changed.

For example:

    ! My Hackedbox style

    root.background: /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/default.jpg
    root.background.mode: center

    menu.title.font: Hack-14
    menu.frame.font: Hack-10

    menu.title: raised gradient diagonal
    menu.title.color: rgb:90/94/98
    menu.title.colorTo: rgb:20/24/28
    menu.title.textColor: white

    menu.frame: sunken gradient diagonal
    menu.frame.color: rgb:10/20/30
    menu.frame.colorTo: rgb:70/80/90
    menu.frame.textColor: rgb:90/a0/b0

    menu.hilite: raised gradient diagonal
    menu.hilite.color: rgb:90/94/98
    menu.hilite.colorTo: rgb:20/24/28
    menu.hilite.textColor: green

    borderColor: green
    bevelWidth: 2
    borderWidth: 1
    handleWidth: 5

Resources that are not specified retain their default values.

This makes it possible to build a style incrementally instead of copying
an existing style and modifying every setting.


Installing a User Style
=======================

Save the style in:

    ~/.hackedbox/styles/

For example:

    ~/.hackedbox/styles/MyStyle

The style can then be selected through the Hackedbox menu.

System-wide styles installed by Hackedbox are normally located in:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/styles/


Reconfiguring
=============

After modifying a style, reload or reconfigure Hackedbox so that the new
resources are read.

If a resource appears to have no effect, verify the resource name and value
against the currently supported style resources. Hackedbox ignores resources
that are not used by the component being configured.


Complete Example
================

The following is a compact example containing the major components of a
style:

    ! Example Hackedbox style

    root.background: /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/default.jpg
    root.background.mode: center

    menu.title.font: Hack-14
    menu.frame.font: Hack-10

    menu.title: raised gradient diagonal
    menu.title.color: rgb:90/94/98
    menu.title.colorTo: rgb:20/24/28
    menu.title.textColor: white

    menu.frame: sunken gradient diagonal
    menu.frame.color: rgb:10/20/30
    menu.frame.colorTo: rgb:70/80/90
    menu.frame.textColor: rgb:90/a0/b0

    menu.hilite: raised gradient diagonal
    menu.hilite.color: rgb:90/94/98
    menu.hilite.colorTo: rgb:20/24/28
    menu.hilite.textColor: green

    window.justify: center

    borderColor: green
    bevelWidth: 2
    borderWidth: 1
    handleWidth: 5

This example is intentionally minimal. A complete style can add focused
and unfocused window textures, labels, buttons, handles, grips, and other
component-specific resources as required.
